  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "None; full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study tests whether sparse dictionary features in LLM representations capture interpretable information and whether a feature can encode opposing concepts, using SAE training, probing and steering tasks, and a qualitative bidirectional-feature case study. This is a conceptual test using a different method from the toy-model superposition work.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports SAE probe and steering outcomes and a qualitative bidirectional-feature case study. It also discusses prior work on feature instability and multidimensionality, but those cited findings are not this study's results. No numerical result is reported for Figure 1.",
    "test_reason": "This is an SAE architecture paper. It trains TopK, JumpReLU and its own AbsTopK SAEs on four LLMs and reports benchmark scores comparing them. No score is set against a baseline that could show sparse features exist or are recovered, such as raw neurons, random SAEs or a dense control. So the probing numbers measure SAE-variant performance, not the superposition claim. The bidirectional man/woman feature is one qualitative example about activation sign, not about whether features are 1-D or multi-dimensional. The fragmentation argument is theoretical (from the non-negativity constraint) and is not measured in conventional SAEs.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors also claim AbsTopK matches or surpasses Difference-in-Means steering (Table 1): it retains more MMLU, with HarmBench comparable or slightly lower. They frame non-negativity as a core cause of feature fragmentation.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study trains and evaluates sparse autoencoders on LLM residual activations, comparing nonnegative TopK and JumpReLU variants with the proposed signed-activation AbsTopK. It does not reproduce the original toy-network method.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Results vary by model, selected layer, SAE variant, and evaluation metric; the paper also varies sparsity k for unsupervised metrics.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "One AbsTopK feature (15741) is shown activating positively on 'man' and negatively on 'woman' in minimal sentence pairs. The authors also argue, from the non-negativity constraint rather than by measurement, that conventional SAEs must split such axes into two redundant features.",
        "proposed_claim": "Sign-constrained SAEs fragment bidirectional concept axes into separate features, so SAE feature counts and identities depend on the activation's sign constraint (bearing on V5 canonicity if measured).",
        "row_ids": [
          "r25"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), 2025",
    "problems": "The bidirectional feature result is a single qualitative case study with no stated base model or numerical activation values. The reported probing results cover selected layers and task domains; no uncertainty estimates are given. The paper's discussion of feature instability and multidimensionality cites other work rather than testing those issues itself.",
    "rows_left_out": "Omitted the reported Unlearning, Absorption, SCR, TPP, and RAVEL task scores and the MMLU/HarmBench steering results because they do not directly measure the sparse-feature recovery or one-dimensional bidirectional-feature behavior named by the tested versions. Omitted figure-only unsupervised metric curves because the text does not provide their plotted values.",
